    "technique_notes": [
      {
        "heading": "Tuning: E\u266d Standard (Half Step Down)",
        "body": "Tune every string DOWN a half step: low E\u2192Eb, A\u2192Ab, D\u2192Db, G\u2192Gb, B\u2192Bb, high e\u2192Eb. SRV used this tuning throughout most of his catalog \u2014 he fingers everything in familiar E-shape positions but the guitar sounds a half step lower than written. Without this, your phrases will clash with any recording."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Chord-Melody Attack: Arpeggio from Bass to High Melody",
        "body": "Lenny's signature move is rolling up from the bass note of an E-shape barre chord to the high melody note \u2014 not a strum, not a full chord attack. Use your pick or thumb to initiate from string 6 (or 4 depending on the inversion), and let each note ring into the next so it blooms into a chord rather than a chord struck all at once. The trap is rushing the roll and collapsing it into a strum \u2014 aim for a lazy, deliberate fan that takes up roughly a full beat."
      },
      {
        "heading": "b3\u21923 Slide: The Blues Curl in E-Shape Position",
        "body": "The core expressive move here is sliding from the minor 3rd to the major 3rd within the E-shape barre position \u2014 on the G string (string 3), that's fret 5 to fret 7 (or 7 to 9 in the C-shape area). Keep your fretting finger relaxed and let the slide glide in one fluid motion rather than hopping. If the pitch sounds flat on arrival, you're releasing finger pressure mid-slide \u2014 maintain even downward weight all the way to the target fret."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Bend-Release on the G String",
        "body": "Several phrases end with a 1-step bend on the G string (string 3) that releases back down \u2014 this is the 'sob' of the phrase. Support the bending finger with the fingers behind it; bending with one finger alone loses control and intonation. The release must land cleanly on the fretted pitch, not hang somewhere between \u2014 slow it down and listen for the pitch locking in on the way back."
      },
      {
        "heading": "E-Shape Barre Voicings: Position Geography",
        "body": "Lenny moves the entire E-shape vocabulary up the neck \u2014 Bb at fret 6, C at fret 8. The barre index locks the root on string 6 while fingers 2, 3, and 4 hold the inner triad shape unchanged. The common trap is letting the barre collapse at strings 1 and 2 \u2014 roll your index finger slightly toward the nut (toward its bony edge) for even pressure across all six strings."
      }
    ],
